## The idea

Renaming must be **one-for-one in both directions**: every s-letter always becomes the same t-letter, and no two s-letters become the same t-letter. So keep two maps and check both.

## Step by step

1. Different lengths → false.
2. For each position: if s[i] already maps to something other than t[i] → false.
3. If t[i] is already the image of a different s-letter → false.
4. Record both mappings.

## Worked example

**Input:** s = "badc", t = "baba"

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| b → b | ok |
| a → a | ok |
| d → b | forward is fine, but b is already the image of b |
| stop | false |

**Result:** false — one map alone would have said true

## Cost

O(n) time, O(alphabet) memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 205 — can the letters of s be renamed, one-for-one, to give t?
public static class Isomorphic {
    public static bool Check(string s, string t) {
        if (s == null || t == null) return s == t;
        if (s.Length != t.Length) return false;
        // Two maps, because the renaming must work both ways: two letters of s may not become the same letter of t.
        var forward = new Dictionary<char, char>();
        var backward = new Dictionary<char, char>();
        for (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {
            char a = s[i], b = t[i];
            if (forward.TryGetValue(a, out char f) && f != b) return false;
            if (backward.TryGetValue(b, out char g) && g != a) return false;
            forward[a] = b;
            backward[b] = a;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why two maps?**

"badc" vs "baba": the forward map alone accepts it, but d and b would both become b.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time, O(alphabet) memory.

**Q: Shared state?**

None.

**Q: Group a million words by pattern.**

Turn each word into its pattern ("paper" → 0,1,0,2,3) and group by that key — InterviewDB Q11's signature idea.

**Q: Related?**

Word Pattern (LeetCode 290) — words instead of characters.

